This rule will detect lifecycle hook methods defined in `.vue` components and check if they modify internal component state or if they call methods that modify internal component state, based on the provided options. You can adjust the `allowInternalStateChange` and `onlyDetectMethodCalls` values depending on your project's preferences.

Note: The `utils/lifecycle-hooks` module (`./utils/lifecycle-hooks.js`) is not included in this example, but it should contain a function (`isLifecycleHook`) to identify lifecycle hooks based on the Vue.js spec. You can find a sample implementation in [eslint-plugin-vue](https://github.com/vuejs/eslint-plugin-vue/tree/master/src/rules/component-lifecycle-hook).

Eine benutzerdefinierte ESLint Regel zur Überwachung von aktualisierten Lifecycle-Hook Methoden in Vue.js, die den internen Komponentenstatus verändern, sowie die Überprüfung, ob Methoden andere Methoden aufrufen, die den internen Komponentenstatus verändern, lautet:

```json
{
  "rules": {
    "vue-update-lifecycle-hooks": "error",
  },
  "plugins": ["eslint-plugin-vue"]
}
```

```javascript
module.exports = {
  rules: {
    "vue/update-lifecycle-hooks": {
      meta: {
        docs: {
          description: "Disallows lifecycle hooks updates that alter the internal component state",
          category: "Best Practices",
        },
        fixable: "code",
        schema: [],
      },

      create(context) {
        return {
          "ExpressionStatement:exit"(node) {
            // Check if the current node is a lifecycle hook function
            const { type, key: { name } } = node.expression;
            if (
              type !== "FunctionExpression" &&
              !vueLifecycleHooks.some((hook) => hook === name)
            ) {
              return;
            }

            // Check if the function body alters the internal component state
            const { body } = node.expression;
            const alteredComponentState = isInternalComponentStateAlteration(body);

            // Check if the function calls another method that alters the internal component state
            const calledMethod = getCalledMethod(body);
            const callsAnotherMethodThatAltersState =
              calledMethod && isInternalComponentStateAlteration(calledMethod);

            if (alteredComponentState || callsAnotherMethodThatAltersState) {
              context.report({
                node,
                message:
                  "Lifecycle hook function does not support modifying internal component state directly or indirectly.",
                fix(fixer) {
                  if (alteredComponentState) {
                    return alterFunctionBody(fixer, body);
                  } else if (callsAnotherMethodThatAltersState) {
                    return createCallFixer(
                      fixer,
                      body,
                      alterCalledMethod.bind(null, calledMethod)
                    );
                  }
                },
              });
            }
          },
        };
      },
    },
  },
};
```

### Details:

- The rule `vue-update-lifecycle-hooks` is set to an error level to enforce its usage.

- The rule relies on the `eslint-plugin-vue` via the `"plugins": ["eslint-plugin-vue"]` option in the `.eslintrc.json` file.

- The `create()` method defines the JavaScript AST traversal logic for the rule which runs each time a new `ExpressionStatement` node with a lifecycle hook function is encountered (identified by type `FunctionExpression` and a specific key name corresponding to each one of Vue's lifecycle hooks list, e.g. `beforeCreate", "created", "beforeMount", "mounted", ...`).

- Within the `create()` method, the `ExpressionStatement:exit` handler checks whether the current node has a function expression with a matching key name.

- The function bodies of identified lifecycle hook functions are then checked using two separate checks:

  - The `isInternalComponentStateAlteration()` function could be implemented to identify any mutations of data, props or computed properties. An example could be:

    ```javascript
    function isInternalComponentStateAlteration(node) {
      if (
        node.type === "AssignmentExpression" &&
        node.left.type === "MemberExpression" &&
        node.left.object.type === "ThisExpression"
      ) {
        const property = node.left.property.name;
        if (VueInternalsDataProps.some((prop) => prop === property)) {
          return true;
        }
      }
      return false;
    }
    ```

  - The `getCalledMethod()` function extracts the called method within the current function body, if any. It could be implemented as follows:

    ```javascript
    function getCalledMethod(node) {
      if (node.type === "CallExpression") {
        return node.callee;
      }
      if (node.type === "BinaryExpression" && node.operator === "&&") {
        return getCalledMethod(node.left);
      }
      return null;
    }
    ```

  - If either of the previous checks identify an alteration of internal component state directly in the current lifecycle hook function or an indirect alteration by calling a method that performs such an alteration, ESLint emits a warning at the identified node.

  - The `fix()` method is used to propose a refactoring of the offending lifecycle hook function body, either by eliminating the state-altering expression or by converting the call to a method altering the internal component state into a computed property assignment that triggers a re-render.

    - The `alterFunctionBody()` function takes a fixer object and the original body AST node, and provides a new body with the state-altering expression replaced with a noop, as suggested by `eslint-plugin-vue`'s `generatedFixer` utility. 

      ```javascript
      function alterFunctionBody(fixer, body) {
        const mutateStatement = body.find(
          ({ type }) => type === "ExpressionStatement"
        );
        return fixer.replaceNode(mutateStatement, fixer.noop());
      }
      ```

    - The `createCallFixer()` function also takes a fixer object and the original body AST node, and provides a new body with a computed property that is assigned the result of the offending method call, triggering a re-render for the computed property to be updated. 

      ```javascript
      function createCallFixer(fixer, body, alterCalledMethod) {
        const { callee, arguments: args } = body.find(
          ({ type }) => type === "CallExpression"
        );
        const newBody = [...body];

        const computedPropertyAssignment = fixer.insertAfter(
          body[0],
          `${fixer.insertTextBefore(
            fixer.insertAfter(callee, ","),
            `computed: {`
          )}${fixer.indentText(
            fixer.insertTextBefore(
              fixer.insertAfter(
                fixer.appendLine(
                  fixer.insertAfter(callee, ","),
                  ") => {\n\tconst result = arguments.length > 0 && arguments[0] !== undefined ? arguments[0] : null;\n"
                ),
                "}"
              ),
              `return result;\n}`.trim()
            ),
            "  "
          )}`
        );

        newBody.splice(body.length - 1, 0, computedPropertyAssignment);
        alterCalledMethod(callee);

        return newBody;
      }
      ```

      Within `createCallFixer()`, a new computed property is added with a default value of `null`. The generated dynamic function defaults to using the first argument (the offending method call) or `null` if there are no arguments. The actual logic might differ based on the specific method and its arguments. After the new computed property is added, the offending method call is replaced with a returning the value of the new computed property.

      The `alterCalledMethod()` function is a closure provided via the `createCallFixer()` function, which is used to replace the offending method call with a reference to the newly added computed property.

      ```javascript
      function alterCalledMethod(callee) {
        return fixer.replaceNode(callee, { object: { name: "computed" } });
      }
      ```

### Caveat:

This implementation is a proof of concept and could be improved, extended, or adjusted based on your specific needs and constraints. The `isInternalComponentStateAlteration()` function could be customized to fit other libraries or frameworks or handle edge cases. Additionally, this implementation assumes that calling methods that alter the internal component state via a lifecycle hook function is an antipattern in your project. If this isn't the case for you, you might want to adjust the rule's enforcement level or logic.

Um eine benutzerdefinierte ESLint-Regel für Vue.js zu schreiben, die die gewünschten Anforderungen erfüllt, können Sie die folgenden Schritte befolgen:

1. Erstellen einer Einstellungsdatei `.eslintrc.js` zum Konfigurieren von ESLint

   Erstellen Sie eine neue Datei unter dem Projektpfad, die den Namen `.eslintrc.js` trägt. Während eines Standard Projekts verwaltet Vue CLI diese Datei für Sie, daher können Sie einfach im Folgenden Code block den untenstehenden Code kopieren und die Datei speichern.

   ```javascript
   module.exports = {
     extends: ['plugin:vue/baseline']
   };
   ```

2. Installieren der `eslint-plugin-vue`-Erweiterung

   Zur Erstellung von benutzerdefinierten Regeln benötigen Sie eine Erweiterung, die das Regelsystem von ESLint für Vue.js umsetzt. Installieren Sie die `eslint-plugin-vue`-Erweiterung mittels folgendem Befehl:

   ```
   npm install eslint-plugin-vue --save-dev
   ```

3. Erstellen einer benutzerdefinierten Regel

   Erstellen Sie eine Datei `rules.js` in Ihrem Projekt-Pfad, in der Sie die benutzerdefinierte Regel definieren:

   ```javascript
   module.exports = {
     rules: {
       'vue/component-changing-state': 'error',
       'vue/component-methods-internal-state-changed': 'warn',
     },
   };
   ```

   Diese Regel definiert zwei eigene Regeln:
   - `vue/component-changing-state`: Erklärt ein Fehler, wenn ein Lifecycle-Hook oder eine Methode eine Änderung des inneren Component-State bewirkt, was im Allgemeinen unerwartet und unbeholfen ist.
   - `vue/component-methods-internal-state-changed`: Wählt eine Warnung aus, wenn eine Methode, die nicht ein Lifecycle-Hook ist, Änderungen des inneren Component-State bewirkt.

4. Implementieren der benutzerdefinierten Regeln

   Öffnen Sie die `.eslintrc.js`-Datei, die Sie zuvor erstellt haben, und fügen Sie die folgenden Zeilen hinzu:

   ```javascript
   module.exports = {
     extends: ['plugin:vue/baseline'],
     plugins: ['vue'],
     rules: {
       // Import our newly created rules file
       ...require('./rules'),
     },
   };
   ```

   Hier importieren Sie die benutzerdefinierte Regel aus der `rules.js`-Datei.

5. Regulärer Ausdruck für die Identifikation von Lifecycle-Hooks und Methoden, die den Status verändern

   Um Leben ins Vorher definierten Regelset zu bringen, sehen wir uns jetzt der Implementierung des regulären Ausdrucks für die Identifikation von Lifecycle-Hooks und Methoden, die den Status verändern, zu.

   ```javascript
   const componentChangingState = {
     'beforeCreate': true,
     'created': true,
     'beforeMount': true,
     'mounted': true,
     'beforeUpdate': true,
     'updated': true,
     'beforeDestroy': true,
     'destroyed': true,
     'activated': true,
     'deactivated': true,
     'errorCaptured': true,
   };

   const componentChangingInternalState = {
     'data': true,
     'computed': true,
     'methods': (method, methodName) => {
       const methodBody = method.body.body;
       if (methodBody.some(bodyNode => {
         // Use Lodash's `get` function to traverse object properties without throwing errors
         return lodash.get(bodyNode, 'left.property.name') === 'state' ||
               lodash.get(bodyNode, 'right.property.name') === 'state';
       })) {
         return true;
       }
       return false;
     }
   };

   module.exports = {
     rules: {
       'vue/component-changing-state': 'error',
       'vue/component-methods-internal-state-changed': 'warn',
       'vue/component-changing-state': error => {
         if (!componentChangingState[error.node.key.name]) {
           return;
         }
         error.message =
             `Avoid changing component state in "${error.node.key.name}" lifecycle hook. ` +
             `This can lead to unintended side effects. Consider using computed properties, data watchers, or methods instead.`;
       },
       'vue/component-methods-internal-state-changed': warn => {
         const methodNode = warn.node.parent;
         if (componentChangingState[methodNode.key.name]) {
           return;
         }

         // Check if a method directly modifies a state property
         const methodName = methodNode.key.name;
         if (componentChangingInternalState.methods(methodNode, methodName)) {
           warn.message = `Method "${methodName}" modifies internal component state directly.` +
             `Consider using computed properties, data watchers, or separate methods instead.`;
         }
       }
     },
   };
   ```

   In der Regel `componentChangingState` verwaltet einen Hash von Lifecycle-Hook Eigenschaften, die Änderungen am internen Komponentenstatus bewirken. In der Regel `componentChangingInternalState.methods` implementiert einen regulären Ausdruck, der Methoden überprüft, ob sie direkte Änderungen am internen Komponentenstatus vornehmen.

6. (Optional) Ausführen von ESLint um sicherzustellen, dass die Regeln richtig funktionieren

   Um sicherzustellen, dass die Regeln ohne Fehler funktionieren, führen Sie den folgenden Befehl im Terminal aus:

   ```bash
   npx eslint --ext .vue,.js src/
   ```

   Dieser Befehl ruft ESLint auf, um alle `.vue` und `.js` Dateien im `src` Punkt zu überprüfen, und alle Meldungen zeigt es.


